#^X_Loc_Precision: [metric used to quantify the Precision associated with the estimation of the X-axis localization of this bright Spot. Different methods might be used. The Cramer-Rao Lower and Upper Bounds methods is widely accepted, but it tends to overestimate the Precision value. Alternatively, the Thompson method, by which Precision is estimated to be proportional to Photon Count, can also be used even though this method highly overestimates the Precision. Whatever method is used, description of how this metric was computed and of the Software that was employed must be provided in the header of the table. Such description must contain enough details to allow interpretation and reproducibility; use if relevant]
#^Y_Loc_Precision: [metric used to quantify the Precision associated with the estimation of the Y-axis localization of this bright Spot. Different methods might be used. The Cramer-Rao Lower and Upper Bounds methods is widely accepted, but it tends to overestimate the Precision value. Alternatively, the Thompson method, by which Precision is estimated to be proportional to Photon Count, can also be used even though this method highly overestimates the Precision. Whatever method is used, description of how this metric was computed and of the Software that was employed must be provided in the header of the table. Such description must contain enough details to allow interpretation and reproducibility; use if relevant]
#^Z_Loc_Precision: [metric used to quantify the Precision associated with the estimation of the Z-axis localization of this bright Spot. Different methods might be used. The Cramer-Rao Lower and Upper Bounds methods is widely accepted, but it tends to overestimate the Precision value. Alternatively, the Thompson method, by which Precision is estimated to be proportional to Photon Count, can also be used even though this method highly overestimates the Precision. Whatever method is used, description of how this metric was computed and of the Software that was employed must be provided in the header of the table. Such description must contain enough details to allow interpretation and reproducibility; use if relevant]
#^Photon_Count: [number of photons detected for the given SM Localization event or Spot; use if relevant]
#^Goodness_of_Fit: [metric quantifying how well the fitted point-spread-function (PSF) model matches the observed fluorescence signal during sub-pixel localization of the single-molecule emission event; use if relevant]
#^Centroid_Intensity: [the signal intensity of the pixel occupying the center-of-mass within a bright Spot (i.e. centroid); use this  if relevant]
#^Peak_Intensity: [the signal intensity of the brightest pixel within a bright Spot (i.e. local maximum); use if relevant]
#^Raw_X: [the Raw sub-pixel X coordinate of this bright Spot relative to the optical system (i.e., Objective and Detector), as determined before any performed post-processing correction procedures (i.e. drift correction, chromatic correction etc); use if relevant]
#^Raw_Y: [the Raw sub-pixel Y coordinate of this bright Spot relative to the optical system (i.e., Objective and Detector), as determined before any performed post-processing correction procedures (i.e. drift correction, chromatic correction etc); use if relevant]
#^Raw_Z: [the Raw sub-pixel Z coordinate of this bright Spot relative to the optical system (i.e., Objective and Detector), as determined before any performed post-processing correction procedures (i.e. drift correction, chromatic correction etc); use if relevant]
#^X_Drift: [this field captures the offset in the observed X-coordinate of the Intensity maxima or the Intensity centre of gravity of the bright Spot when comparing the Observed vs. Expected (i.e., based on a fiducial reference) positions. This shall be calculates as: √(Xe - Xo)^2, and reported in physical distance using the unit indicated in the header; use if relevant]
#^Y_Drift: [this field captures the offset in the observed Y-coordinate of the Intensity maxima or the Intensity centre of gravity of the bright Spot when comparing the Observed vs. Expected (i.e., based on a fiducial reference) positions. This shall be calculates as: √(Xe - Xo)^2, and reported in physical distance using the unit indicated in the header; use if relevant]
#^Z_Drift: [this field captures the offset in the observed Z-coordinate of the Intensity maxima or the Intensity centre of gravity of the bright Spot when comparing the Observed vs. Expected (i.e., based on a fiducial reference) positions. This shall be calculates as: √(Xe - Xo)^2, and reported in physical distance using the unit indicated in the header; use if relevant]
#^X_Chromatic_Shift: [this field captures the offset in the observed X-coordinate of the Intensity maxima or the Intensity centre of gravity of the bright Spot when comparing the Reference (λR) vs. the Test (λT) wavelengths. This shall be calculated as: √(XλT - XλR)^2. This offset could be reported either in number of Pixels or in physical Distance, when a sub-Pixel offset needs to be calculated; use if relevant]
#^Y_Chromatic_Shift: [this field captures the offset in the observed Y-coordinate of the Intensity maxima or the Intensity centre of gravity of the bright Spot when comparing the Reference (λR) vs. the Test (λT) wavelengths. This shall be calculated as: √(XλT - XλR)^2. This offset could be reported either in number of Pixels or in physical Distance, when a sub-Pixel offset needs to be calculated; use if relevant]
#^Z_Chromatic_Shift: [this field captures the offset in the observed Z-coordinate of the Intensity maxima or the Intensity centre of gravity of the bright Spot when comparing the Reference (λR) vs. the Test (λT) wavelengths. This shall be calculated as: √(XλT - XλR)^2. This offset could be reported either in number of Pixels or in physical Distance, when a sub-Pixel offset needs to be calculated; use if relevant]
#^X_Loc_Error: [metric used to quantify the Error associated with the estimation of the X-axis localization of this bright Spot. Whatever method is used, a description of how this metric was computed and of the Software that was employed must be provided in the header of the table. Such description must contain enough details to allow interpretation and reproducibility; use if relevant]
#^Y_Loc_Error: [metric used to quantify the Error associated with the estimation of the Y-axis localization of this bright Spot. Whatever method is used, a description of how this metric was computed and of the Software that was employed must be provided in the header of the table. Such description must contain enough details to allow interpretation and reproducibility; use if relevant]
#^Z_Loc_Error: [metric used to quantify the Error associated with the estimation of the Z-axis localization of this bright Spot. Whatever method is used, a description of how this metric was computed and of the Software that was employed must be provided in the header of the table. Such description must contain enough details to allow interpretation and reproducibility; use if relevant]
